.TH PROFIL 2
.SH NAME
profil \- execution time profile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int profil(char *buff, int bufsiz, int offset, int scale);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Buff
points to an area of core whose length (in bytes) is given by
.IR bufsiz .
After this call, the user's program counter (pc)
is examined each clock tick.
.B Note:
v7 said a sixtieth of a second.  HZ is
.B 250
here.
.I offset
is subtracted from it, and the result multiplied by
.IR scale .
If the resulting number corresponds to a word
inside
.I buff,
that word is incremented.
.PP
The scale is interpreted as an unsigned,
fixed-point fraction with binary point at the
left: 0177777(8) gives a 1-1 mapping of pc's to words
in
.I buff;
077777(8) maps each pair of instruction words
together.
02(8) maps all instructions onto the beginning of
.I buff
(producing a non-interrupting core clock).
.PP
Profiling is turned off by giving a
.I scale
of 0 or 1.
It is rendered
ineffective by giving a
.I bufsiz
of 0.
Profiling is turned off when an
.I exec
is executed, but remains on in child and parent both
after a
.IR fork .
Profiling may be turned off if an update in
.I buff
would cause a memory fault.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
prof(1)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B There is no profiling runtime here.
.I Profil
is a generated syscall stub like any other and the gate exists, but the
.I monitor
and
.I mcount
half of the machinery \- what v7's page pointed at as
.IR monitor (3),
and what
.B cc \-p
would install \- has not been ported, so nothing calls this.
.PP
The scale wording above is v7's and describes PDP-11 word addresses.  Read it as
a fraction over
.I word
addresses here; a word is one 48-bit unit and an address is a word index.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_profil
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
